        "en": {
          "research_question": "The investigation aims to measure sex-based disparities in reported infectious disease cases across European nations and identify jurisdictions that diverge from continental averages.",
          "study_design": "This observational analysis processed routine surveillance records from a ten-year period to compute sex-specific case proportions and assess geographic variation using distributional statistics.",
          "population_setting": "The dataset comprised notification records from European Union and European Economic Area member states between 2012 and 2021, narrowing the focus to sixteen pathogens that exceeded a defined disease burden threshold.",
          "main_findings": "Notification records consistently indicated higher male proportions for infections such as HIV, hepatitis B, and malaria across all participating regions. In contrast, pathogens including pertussis and chlamydia exhibited female predominance in most areas. Age-stratified variability was most notable for chlamydia and listeriosis, while anomalous data points primarily originated from locations with limited case submissions.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Tracking these consistent reporting patterns supports the characterization of epidemiological trends and may highlight how regional screening approaches or demographic factors intersect with disease surveillance.",
          "limitations": "Anomalous statistical values were predominantly generated by jurisdictions with minimal case submissions. Furthermore, the evaluation depends on administrative notification records, which capture reporting and testing activities rather than direct measures of actual infection rates.",
          "gids_interpretation": "This work supplies a decade-long reference framework for sex-disaggregated pathogen reporting within European public health systems. The documented patterns provide contextual benchmarks for interpreting future surveillance outputs and examining how demographic variables align with established reporting structures.",
